Identify the historical context of Mets fan transportation to Citi Field and determine when bus routes like the Q48 became a common method for fans to attend games.
The historical context of Mets fan transportation to Citi Field includes the use of bus routes such as the Q48, which became a common method for fans to attend games. The Casey Stengel Depot, located across the street from Citi Field, has served as a key hub for buses used on routes like the Q48. This depot was originally inherited from the North Shore Bus Company in 1947 and was rebuilt in the late 1940s, re-opening in 1950. It was rebuilt again in the 1990s, opening on August 16, 1992, and renamed the Casey Stengel Depot. The Q48 route, along with other services, has been utilized by fans to reach Citi Field, particularly after Mets games and events such as the US Open tennis tournament. While the exact date when the Q48 became a common method is not specified, its association with the Casey Stengel Depot and its proximity to Citi Field indicate its long-standing role in facilitating fan transportation.
